23:18:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fantastic point, and the glasses-fogging analogy is exactly why so many people (including well-meaning moms and first-aid instructors) believe that the same thing must happen inside your throat. Let’s tackle it head-on. The short answer is: **Yes, a minuscule amount of water *does* condense on your airway surfaces when you breathe steam—just like on your glasses. But the amount is so small, and your airway handles it so quickly, that it never pools or causes choking.** Here’s why the glasses analogy breaks down when you move inside the body. ### 1. Condensation happens, but the volume is trivial — you already do a version of this every cold day Your glasses fog up because the warm, moist air from your breath (or from the pot) touches a cold lens, and the water vapor condenses into countless tiny droplets. The same physics applies in your upper airway: as hot, saturated air passes through your cooler nose and throat, a thin film of water will indeed condense on the moist lining. But consider what happens when you breathe out on a cold day and see your breath fog. Inside your body, exactly the same condensation happened in reverse — just without you noticing. On a frosty morning, you’re exhaling body-warm, saturated air into the cold. That air left your lungs at 37°C and 100% humidity; as it traveled up your windpipe and hit the slightly cooler trachea and nose, a tiny bit of water condensed *on the inside of you*. You didn’t choke. You just swallowed it without a second thought. That happens with every breath, 24/7, at a much smaller scale. ### 2. The numbers: How much liquid water could possibly condense? Let’s do the math for a worst-case steam facial. You take a deep breath of fully saturated air at, say, 60°C (the maximum you’d tolerate near a boiling pot). Your lung temperature is 37°C. The maximum water-holding capacity of air drops by about 20 grams per cubic meter between those two temperatures. A large breath is about 0.5